+### Robodebt in the public record
+
+[Victoria Legal Aid’s account](https://www.legalaid.vic.gov.au/depth-look-our-robo-debt-test-case), sections on review and both test cases, gives the two recalculations, the later discontinuance, the review figure and the description of the burden. The [Royal Commission report](https://www.royalcommission.gov.au/robodebt/report), Chapter 10, reproduces the Amato declarations and traces the direction to staff. [Justice Kyrou’s 18 March 2024 presentation](https://www.fedcourt.gov.au/digital-law-library/judges-speeches/justice-kyrou/kyrou-j-20240318), section on the scheme and the AAT, gives the findings on Carney, later decisions and the May 2018 recommendation, with pinpoints to the Commission report. The publication recommendation is Chapter 20, recommendation 20.4. These sources were checked on 21 November 2026.
+
+### Sources for this working edition, by chapter
+
+These notes show what each source contributes. See the [alphabetical references](#references) for publication details. The separate project file [SOURCE-MAP.md](SOURCE-MAP.md) records which passages were consulted and when.
+
+#### Boundary critique
+
+Chapters 1 and 8 use the boundary questions in [Ulrich’s primer](https://wulrich.com/csh.html) and [Ulrich and Reynolds (2010)](https://oro.open.ac.uk/21489/) to ask who benefits, who can decide and whose account can challenge the boundary.
+
+#### Observation and active vision
+
+Chapter 2 uses [Parr and Friston (2017)](https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2017.0376), §§1, 3 and 6, for the pace of rechecking. [Parr et al. (2021)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/neurorobotics/articles/10.3389/fnbot.2021.651432/full), Figure 7, illustrates how a different view can discriminate between possibilities. Both comparisons concern visual search.
+
+#### Learning through interaction
+
+The soil check in Chapter 2 draws on [De Jaegher and Di Paolo (2007)](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11097-007-9076-9) on what people can make sense of together and what each participant can later do.
+
+#### Exit and voice
+
+Chapters 3 and 9 draw on [Hirschman (1970)](https://books.google.com/books?id=vYO6sDvjvcgC) to follow the practical conditions for leaving and for making a challenge heard.
+
+#### Shared records and commitments
+
+Chapter 4's rota compares the different uses of a shared record in [Star and Griesemer (1989)](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/030631289019003001) and [Star (2010)](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0162243910377624) with the work required to deliver a commitment in [Ing (2008)](https://coevolving.com/pubs/2008_Systemist_v30_n2_p154_Ing_Offerings-Language-Action.pdf), pp. 162–164.
+
+#### Real opportunities and affordances
+
+Chapter 5 draws on [Sen (1979)](https://tannerlectures.org/documents/sen80/), §4, and [Robeyns and Byskov (2025)](https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2025/entries/capability-approach/), §§2.2–2.3, for real opportunities and conversion factors. [Gibson (1979)](https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315740218), chapter 8, informs the door example. The account of secure conditions draws on the chapter abstract and the associated SEP discussion of [Byskov, Kramm and Östlund (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-55897-9_14). The ordinary-reserves test draws on the unpublished *ME Final Eight* project notes.
+
+#### Time and lead time
+
+Chapter 6 takes measured time and occasion from [Ing and Metcalf (2024)](https://coevolving.com/pubs/2024_JISSS_v67_a4154_ReframingSystemsThinkingForSystemsChanges.pdf) and their citation of [Orlikowski and Yates (2002)](https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.13.6.684.501), p. 686. [Ing (2022)](https://www.iiisci.org/Journal/PDV/sci/pdfs/IP164LL22.pdf) supplies a parallel about practice and rhythms. [Haasnoot et al. (2013)](https://ris.utwente.nl/ws/files/247793059/Haasnoot2013dynamic.pdf), §5, informs the lead-time comparison.
+
+#### Thresholds for each person
+
+Chapters 7 and 8 draw on the per-person threshold in [Nussbaum (2003)](https://doi.org/10.1080/1354570022000077926). The example gives the library course a specific promise. The floor, trade-off and conflict distinctions draw on the earlier *Value Spine* and *ME Final Eight* project notes.
+
+#### Independent witnesses and shared error
+
+The shared-recording example and model illustration in Chapter 9 follow [Chernets (2026)](https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7390698), §§1.1 and 5. The design-effect illustration assumes the same error correlation for each pair of sources. Chernets credits [Kish (1965)](https://books.google.com/books?id=yoppAAAAMAAJ) for the design effect. [Kohli (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29800) applies it to model judges, and [Kim et al. (2025)](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v267/kim25e.html) measure correlated model errors. [Hossain et al. (2026)](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.22512v1) and [Morante and Romero García-Rubio (2026)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44564-026-00040-z) provide further task-specific comparisons.
+
+#### Review, selection and authority
+
+Chapter 9 compares what reaches a decision with [Van der Maden et al. (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3772318.3791069) on evaluation and action, [Chang and Zhang (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12705), §§2–4, on selection into a screened set, [Paxino et al. (2026)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43006860/) on discharge communication, and [Törnberg (2026)](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17114v1), Table 1, on review and decision authority in a proposed institution.
+
+#### Recovery after conditions change
+
+Chapter 10 uses [Woods (2015)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ress.2015.03.018), §§2.1–2.4, to check capacity for the next breakdown. [Binder et al. (2026)](https://journals.plos.org/climate/article?id=10.1371/journal.pclm.0000982) give a comparison from energy transitions about what stays changed after reversal.
+
+#### Stewardship and handover
+
+Chapter 11 recovers the Saturday Workshop from the released 0.8 reader and the stewardship and burden passages in the earlier *Value Spine* and *ME Final Eight*. [Orr (1996)](https://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7591/j.ctt1hhfnkz) offers a comparison on how technicians share repair knowledge.
+
+#### Collective capabilities and caring institutions
+
+Chapter 11 draws on [Robeyns (2017)](https://doi.org/10.11647/OBP.0130), §§3.6 and 4.6, for different kinds of collective provision, and on [Tronto (2010)](https://doi.org/10.1080/17496535.2010.484259) for institutional care. [Ostrom (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19628857/) and [Li and Ma (2026)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s41257-026-00166-w) inform the questions about who contributes, whose observations count and who can decide. The chosen-commitment scene draws on the earlier *After-Fall Reader*.
+
+#### Apprenticeship and transfer
+
+The soil and workshop examples compare practice and transfer with [Collins, Brown and Newman (1987)](https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED284181.pdf) and [Blume et al. (2010)](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0149206309352880). [Billett (2001)](https://doi.org/10.1108/EUM0000000005548), [Edmondson (1999)](https://dash.harvard.edu/entities/publication/13a7b031-0fdd-45ec-a7e0-2b80e2bc679f) and [Ten Cate et al. (2016)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26630606/) inform how to check support, speaking up and responsibility.
+
+#### Assisted results and later skill
+
+In high-school maths, [Bastani et al. (2025)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40560616/) found that how AI help was designed changed performance after the help was removed. [Wiles et al. (2026)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-026-02566-6) found no detectable gain on later unaided technical questions after an assisted work task. [Contractor and Reyes (2026)](https://iza.org/publications/dp/18792/experimental-evidence-on-the-learning-impact-of-generative-ai) reported knowledge gains still evident one week later in their essay-based experiment. [Wei (2026)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.2029407/full) provides a further comparison. The [Diener et al. (2026)](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.06346v2) model, §§5–6, explores how practice and help may affect skill over time.
+
+#### Cynefin and complex change
+
+Chapter 12 takes context-sensitive action from [Kurtz and Snowden (2003)](https://doi.org/10.1147/SJ.423.0462), the [Cynefin Company overview](https://thecynefin.co/about-us/about-cynefin-framework/) and the [Cynefin Wiki](https://cynefin.io/wiki/Cynefin). [Snowden (2022)](https://thecynefin.co/estuarine-mapping/), steps 1–7, informs the constraint mapping. [Ejsing (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsci.2026.104439) examines what a tipping-point account can obscure. The junction is an illustrative case.
+
+#### How group capacities arise
+
+Chapter 12 uses [Gordon (2023)](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ede.12439) and [McMillen and Levin (2024)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-024-06037-4) as comparisons for the levels where a group response can be observed.
+
+#### Enabling constraints and autonomy
+
+[Juarrero (2023)](https://doi.org/10.7551/mitpress/14630.001.0001), chapter 6, informs the bus example. [Froese and Ziemke (2009)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.artint.2008.12.001), [Levin (2019)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02688/full), [Levin (2022)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/systems-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnsys.2022.768201/full), [Froese and Taguchi (2019)](https://www.mdpi.com/2409-9287/4/2/14) and [Kiverstein et al. (2022)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9260223/) inform where to look for the conditions that sustain a capacity.
+
+#### Warnings and recurring anomalies
+
+[Vaughan (1996, enlarged in 2015)](https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/C/bo22781921.html) examines how recurring anomalies were accepted before Challenger. [Leveson (2004)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S092575350300047X) gives a systems approach to control and feedback.
+
+#### Earlier Mechanical Ethics designs
+
+Chapter 14 develops the seven designs from the released 0.8 edition's *What This Can Build*.
